  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Kenya &#x2013; Silage production</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">With a  DGGF Start-up Loan FIT Ned B.V. (FIT) will be able, together with the local daughter, to increase the silage production capacity and expand the existing supply through the production and sale of protein-rich concentrates (also known as additive or dietary supplement).The company&apos;s focus is on selling corn silage to local farmers with the goal of increasing the cows&apos; milk production. FIT buys corn locally and then turns it into silage. This long-life fermented feed contains a higher nutritional value than ordinary green fodder such as grass or corn, which is now common in Kenya as animal feed. Kenya has the highest per capita milk consumption in Africa, the demand for milk will continue to grow. The problem that FIT wants to tackle through its silage is to increase the low milk production of the cows in Kenya. In the Netherlands, an average cow produces 30 litres of milk per day. Food plays a major role in this. Another problem fit wants to tackle is the low profit margin of farmers.The investment is in machines, vehicles, inventory and operational costs. The expected impact is local employment to support 74 jobs of which 53 are newly created jobs.</narrative>
  </description>
